<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- RemoteViews for the "Distance" data field (see RecordingDistanceDataType.kt).

     Label and value are stacked in a LinearLayout rather than layered in the FrameLayout,
     so on a quarter-height cell the label takes its space off the top and the value gets
     what's left, instead of overlapping the digits.

     (2026-09-10) The top row carries per-camera battery percentages on the left with the
     field label pushed to the right edge (layout_weight on the label, gravity=end).

     (2026-09-11) There is no recording dot here any more. It began as a single
     corner-pinned dot, then became one tinted dot per camera, then a dot stacked under
     each percentage — and at every size it cost horizontal or vertical room this cell does
     not have, which is what was truncating the label. The percentage itself now blinks
     while that camera is recording, which says the same thing in no space at all. Battery
     colour lives on the Recording Control tile; this field is numbers only.

     Three camera slots are declared, matching that tile's stripe cap. Slots for cameras
     that aren't connected are GONE and collapse completely. Blinking toggles between
     VISIBLE and INVISIBLE, never GONE, so a recording camera's neighbours don't shuffle
     sideways twice a second. -->
<FrameLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:id="@+id/recordingDistanceRoot"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:paddingStart="4dp"
    android:paddingEnd="4dp"
    android:paddingTop="1dp"
    android:paddingBottom="1dp">

    <LinearLayout
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:orientation="vertical">

        <LinearLayout
            android:id="@+id/distanceTopRow"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:orientation="horizontal"
            android:gravity="center_vertical">

            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/batteryPct1"
                android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginEnd="6dp"
                android:includeFontPadding="false"
                android:maxLines="1"
                android:fontFamily="sans-serif"
                android:textFontWeight="300"
                android:textScaleX="0.8"
                android:textSize="11sp"
                android:visibility="gone"
                android:text="--%" />

            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/batteryPct2"
                android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginEnd="6dp"
                android:includeFontPadding="false"
                android:maxLines="1"
                android:fontFamily="sans-serif"
                android:textFontWeight="300"
                android:textScaleX="0.8"
                android:textSize="11sp"
                android:visibility="gone"
                android:text="--%" />

            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/batteryPct3"
                android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginEnd="6dp"
                android:includeFontPadding="false"
                android:maxLines="1"
                android:fontFamily="sans-serif"
                android:textFontWeight="300"
                android:textScaleX="0.8"
                android:textSize="11sp"
                android:visibility="gone"
                android:text="--%" />

            <!-- Weight here is what right-justifies the label: it absorbs whatever the
                 percentages don't use, and gravity=end pins the text to the far edge. -->
            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/distanceLabel"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_weight="1"
                android:gravity="end"
                android:includeFontPadding="false"
                android:maxLines="1"
                android:fontFamily="sans-serif"
                android:textFontWeight="700"
                android:textScaleX="0.8"
                android:textSize="11sp"
                android:text="DISTANCE" />

        </LinearLayout>

        <!-- includeFontPadding is false here to match every other TextView in this file.
             It was true, with no reason recorded, and that was the gap: the flag reserves
             the font's full ascent AND descent, and these are digits, which have no
             descender to reserve for. The reserved-but-unused band sat between the label
             row and the number, which is why the spacing looked wider than a native Karoo
             field's.

             THE FONT (2026-09-11, resolved). The value uses "ibm-plex-sans-condensed",
             the genuinely condensed face already installed on the Karoo. Everything
             between 0.1.36 and 0.1.53 was an attempt to imitate it by squashing the normal
             face, and that could never have worked.

             /system/etc/fonts.xml maps "sans-serif" to IBM Plex Sans (100=Thin, 200=Light,
             300=Regular, 400=Medium, 700=SemiBold, 900=Bold — non-standard, and why a
             default TextView renders in Medium here). It also declares:

               <family name="ibm-sans-cond">
                   <font weight="600" style="normal">IBMPlexSansCondensed-Medium.otf</font>
               </family>
               <alias name="ibm-plex-sans-condensed" to="ibm-sans-cond" />

             So the condensed face IS selectable, and textFontWeight must be 600 to match
             what that family declares. Note the file is *Medium* despite the 600 label —
             an earlier revision of this comment assumed 600 meant something SemiBold-heavy
             and passed the family over on that basis. It is one step above Regular, which
             is where the whole search was trying to land.

             Do not reintroduce textScaleX here. It scales the rendered glyph horizontally:
             stems thin by the same factor the width drops while horizontal strokes keep
             full thickness, so a squashed normal face is narrow-and-light, never
             narrow-with-full-stroke. Roughly a dozen settings were judged on the device
             and every one was too wide or too light for exactly that reason. A real
             condensed design has no such trade.

             Two related notes. "sans-serif-condensed" is NOT this face — fonts.xml aliases
             it straight back to plain sans-serif, which is why the 0.1.20-era attempts at
             it were no-ops. And this face needs no fontFeatureSettings="tnum": its digits
             are already tabular, all exactly 540/1000 units wide, so numbers don't shift
             as they tick over. The family carries no tnum feature at all, because there is
             nothing for it to switch to. -->
        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/distanceValue"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="0dp"
            android:layout_weight="1"
            android:gravity="center"
            android:includeFontPadding="false"
            android:maxLines="1"
            android:fontFamily="ibm-plex-sans-condensed"
            android:textFontWeight="600"
            android:textStyle="normal"
            android:textSize="28sp"
            android:text="--" />

    </LinearLayout>

</FrameLayout>
